@juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge README · dsh-codex-bridge：Observe and steer a local **Codex** (OpenAI) session from **DeepSeek Harness**.

The plugin talks to `codex app-server` — the same protocol the Codex app itself
uses — which is what makes the live parts possible at all:

@juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge README · dsh-codex-bridge：It was built as a DSH counterpart to a Codex session running in the Codex desktop
app: read what that session is doing, hand it the next instruction, and know
exactly which parts are impossible rather than guessing.

@juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge README · As a bundle (from npm, or from GitHub)：```bash
npm view @juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge version dist.fileCount dist.unpackedSize dist.shasum
```

@juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge README · From a checkout (the development route)：The package name is `@juyanjy/dsh-codex-bridge` — ours, published on npm. The
unscoped `dsh-codex-bridge` belongs to an unrelated project, so neither the
symlink nor the loader entry uses it. Both install routes resolve the same scoped
package name:
